<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chasseurs d'Afrique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chasseurs d’Afrique</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chasseurs d'Afrique were light cavalry regiments of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Armée d'Afrique</a>, first raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1831 and recruited mainly from Frenchmen and European settlers. They fought in the conquest of Algeria, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-intervention-in-mexico">French intervention in Mexico</a> and both world wars, later as armoured units. The last regiments were disbanded or converted after Algerian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chasseurs-d-afrique.jpg" alt="Chasseurs d'Afrique" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Félix Emmanuel Philippoteaux · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chasseurs_d%27Afrique_%C3%A0_Balaclava.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The corps was formed shortly after the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> by transfers from the chasseurs à cheval, other metropolitan cavalry regiments and some infantry units. At first about 40 men of each squadron were indigenous horsemen recruited locally, but in 1836 the Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> troopers were moved to the newly organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spahis">Spahis</a>, the other main cavalry of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Armée d'Afrique</a>. Two more regiments were raised in 1832 and 1833, and the Chasseurs became the mounted equivalent of the Zouave infantry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Besides many North African campaigns, the regiments served in the Second Italian War of Independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> (1892) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> (1895). In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> the 1st and 4th regiments secured the flank of Lord Cardigan during the Charge of the Light Brigade, advancing rapidly in open order. On 5 May 1863 the 1st regiment fought Mexican lancers at the Battle of San Pablo del Monte, and its flag became the first of any French cavalry regiment to be decorated with the Cross of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour. All four regiments then in existence fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and lost heavily in charges at Sedan.</p><p>Seven regiments, three of them marching regiments, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and squadrons fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and against the Turks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Conversion to mechanised units began in 1933 with White TBC and White-Laffly 50 AM armoured cars and was completed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In 1941 the new 6th and 7th regiments fought Allied forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> during Operation Exporter, and the 5th, with M4 Sherman and M5 light tanks, landed in Provence in August 1944 and fought through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Until 1914 the troopers wore light blue tunics with yellow facings, tucked into a red sash, with red breeches and the taconnet, a light blue and red shako; the blue tunics reportedly earned them the nickname Blue Butchers among some opponents. After the corps ended through disbandments and transfers between 1962 and 1964, the 1st Chasseurs d'Afrique Regiment was re-established in 1998 to preserve its traditions. Stationed at Canjuers, it is a mechanised unit of the French Army that keeps 5 May as its day and has brought back the red sash, and at times the fez, for parade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chasseurs d'Afrique” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French Foreign Legion was founded in 1831 for service there and later made its headquarters at Sidi Bel Abbès, and the colony also produced the Zouaves, the Tirailleurs algériens, the Spahis and the <b>Chasseurs d’Afrique</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
